Notebook[{Cell["Advanced Lesson 15 - Exercises: Contexts\nMikey Ferguson\nMATH 7013 - Advanced Mathematical Modeling", "Title"], Cell["1: What is the current context?", "Section"], Cell[BoxData["$Context"], "Input"], Cell["Under a fresh kernel this is \"Global`\". A context is a namespace: every\nsymbol's full name is its context followed by a backquote followed by its\nshort name, so a symbol typed as x is really Global`x. The backquote is the\ncontext mark and is not an apostrophe.", "Text"], Cell["2: What is the current context path?", "Section"], Cell[BoxData["$ContextPath"], "Input"], Cell["The context path is the list of contexts searched, in order, when a short\nname is typed. It always contains \"System`\", where every built-in lives, and\n\"Global`\". Anything a loaded package has added appears here too - which is\nhow Needs makes a package's exported names available without qualification.", "Text"], Cell["3: A symbol in the current context", "Section"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{RowBox[{"Clear", "[", "newSymbol", "]"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{"newSymbol", "=", "\"Current\""}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"Context", "[", "newSymbol", "]"}], ",", "newSymbol"}], "}"}]}]], "Input"], Cell["The symbol was created in whatever context was current, so its context is\nGlobal` and its full name is Global`newSymbol.", "Text"], Cell["4: The same short name in a different context", "Section"], Cell["The obvious way to do this is to switch context with Begin, assign, and\nswitch back with End. Begin does change the current context, and End\nrestores the previous one and returns the name of the context being left.\nBut the obvious way does not work here, and it is worth watching it fail\nbefore repairing it - the failure is exactly what Exercise 6 is about.", "Text"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{RowBox[{"Begin", "[", "\"newContext`\"", "]"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{"newSymbol", "=", "\"New\""}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{"End", "[", "]"}]}]], "Input"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"Context", "[", "newSymbol", "]"}], ",", "newSymbol", ",", RowBox[{"NameQ", "[", "\"newContext`newSymbol\"", "]"}]}], "}"}]], "Input"], Cell["What went wrong. The symbol is still Global`newSymbol, its value is now\n\"New\", and no symbol called newContext`newSymbol was ever created. Begin did\nswitch the current context, but creating a symbol is not the first thing\nMathematica tries when it reads a short name - it first searches the context\npath, and Global` is on that path. Global`newSymbol already existed from\nExercise 3, so the short name resolved to it and the assignment overwrote it.\nNothing new was created because nothing new was needed.\nThe repair below restores the Global` symbol and creates the other one by\nits full name, which bypasses the search entirely.", "Text"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{RowBox[{"newSymbol", "=", "\"Current\""}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{"newContext`newSymbol", "=", "\"New\""}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"newSymbol", ",", "newContext`newSymbol"}], "}"}]}]], "Input"], Cell["Now the two symbols genuinely coexist. They share a short name and nothing\nelse - different full names, different values, no interference. That is the\nentire purpose of the mechanism: two pieces of code can each have a symbol\ncalled newSymbol without either knowing about the other.\nReal packages avoid the collision a third way. They call Begin[\"`Private`\"]\ninside a BeginPackage block, which removes Global` from the context path\nfor the duration, so short names inside the package cannot reach outside it.", "Text"], Cell["5: Information on both symbols", "Section"], Cell["?Global`newSymbol", "Input"], Cell["?newContext`newSymbol", "Input"], Cell["Each reports its own value: \"Current\" for the one in Global` and \"New\" for\nthe one in newContext`. Asking about the short name alone would report\nwhichever the context path finds first, which is why the full names are used\nhere.", "Text"], Cell["6: Why Remove, and not Clear or ClearAll", "Section"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{RowBox[{"Clear", "[", "newSymbol", "]"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"Context", "[", "newSymbol", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"ValueQ", "[", "newSymbol", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"NameQ", "[", "\"Global`newSymbol\"", "]"}]}], "}"}]}]], "Input"], Cell["Clear removes a symbol's values, and ClearAll removes its values,\ndefinitions, attributes, messages and defaults. Neither removes the symbol.\nThe cell above shows the difference: after Clear, the symbol has no value,\nbut it still exists - it is still known to Global`, and NameQ still finds it.", "Text"], Cell["That residue is what causes the trouble the exercise is asking about.\nSuppose Global`newSymbol already exists and the current context path\nincludes Global`. Typing the short name newSymbol anywhere - including\ninside a Begin block for another context - resolves to the existing\nGlobal` symbol, because a symbol that already exists in a context on the\npath is found before a new one is created. No amount of clearing changes\nthat, since clearing empties the symbol without unregistering the name.\nRemove is the operation that actually deletes the name from its context.\nOnce Global`newSymbol has been removed, the next use of the short name has\nnothing to find and creates a fresh symbol in whichever context is current.\nThat is why accessing \"the same symbol\" in a different context requires\nRemove: the obstruction is the existence of the name, not the presence of a\nvalue, and only Remove addresses existence.", "Text"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{RowBox[{"Remove", "[", "\"Global`newSymbol\"", "]"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{"NameQ", "[", "\"Global`newSymbol\"", "]"}]}]], "Input"], Cell["False - the name is gone. The symbol in newContext` is untouched, since it\nwas never the one in the way.", "Text"]}, WindowSize -> {1100, 850}, StyleDefinitions -> "Default.nb"]
(* End of Notebook Content *)
